<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louw Wepener</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1812">1812</a>–<a href="/years/1865">1865</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Lourens Jacobus Wepener</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louw Wepener was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> commandant of the Orange Free State. Born in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a>, he led Free State <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a> in the war of 1865 against Moshoeshoe's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sotho-people">Basotho</a> and was killed in August 1865 while leading an assault on the mountain stronghold of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thaba-bosiu">Thaba Bosiu</a>. The town of Wepener in the Free State is named after him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louw-wepener.jpg" alt="Louw Wepener" width="320" height="478" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Delicia Van Rooyen · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louw_Wepener_Grave_Bethulie03.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lourens Jacobus Wepener was born on 21 July 1812 in Graaf-Reinet, the son of a German immigrant, Frederick Jacobus Wepener, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a> woman, Johanna Maria Erasmus. He lived with his uncle Lourens and was christened by the Reverend Andrew Murray of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-reformed-church">Dutch Reformed Church</a>. He married first Hester Susanna Nel and later Hilletje Maria Levina Van Aardt, with whom he had nine children.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Wepener took part in several of the Frontier Wars of 1834–1853 as acting field cornet. In 1850 he moved to the farm De Nek near Aliwal North, fought in the Eighth Frontier War of 1850–1853 against the Xhosa and was promoted to commandant for his leadership. He later moved to the Orange Free State, bought the farms <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salamis-cyprus">Constantia</a> and Moordernaarspoort in the Bethulie district and was appointed acting commandant.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/free-state-basotho-wars">Free State–Basotho Wars</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moshoeshoe-i">Moshoeshoe I</a>, which won the Free State large tracts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sotho-people">Basotho</a> land, the Free State forces were led by Commandant-General Johan Fick and Commandant Wepener. After a first attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thaba-bosiu">Thaba Bosiu</a> failed on 8 August 1865, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a> tried again on 15 August. As Wepener and his agterryer climbed the Khubelu pass, Basotho soldiers spotted them and he was shot dead on the spot. About 11 Boers were killed and about 30 wounded.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Two of his men, Carl Mathey and Chris du Randt, went up at night to find his body and placed it in a shallow trench. It was reported that Moshoeshoe ordered his warriors to cut out and eat Wepener's heart, believing they would gain his courage. He was first buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thaba-bosiu">Thaba Bosiu</a> by Dr Prosper Lautre of the Paris Evangelical Mission Society. Eleven months later his son Dick Wepener came with four friends to collect the remains, guided by Moshoeshoe's son Tladi, and reburied them on the family farm at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salamis-cyprus">Constantia</a>.</p><p>The Khubelu pass is also called Wepener's pass. The town of Wepener, founded in 1867 on the banks of the Jammersbergspuit, a tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caledon-river">Caledon River</a>, has a monument to him outside its town hall, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-african-defence-force">South African Defence Force</a> named the Louw Wepener decoration and medal after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Louw Wepener” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the war of 1865–1866 the commandos failed to storm the mountain fortress of Thaba Bosiu, where the commandant <b>Louw Wepener</b> was killed in 1865, but they devastated Basotho fields and herds and forced Moshoeshoe to cede land in the Treaty of Thaba Bosiu in 1866.</blockquote><a href="/empire/orange-free-state">Orange Free State · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graaff-reinet">Graaff-Reinet</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thaba-bosiu">Thaba Bosiu</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-32.25,24.54&amp;year=1846" aria-label="Graaff-Reinet on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5181 3336 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Louw Wepener"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5631 3606h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5631 3606h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-32.25,24.54&amp;year=1846">What was here in 1846? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
